              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *bhakṣaṇaṁ*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the four food words from narrowest to widest",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the four food words from narrowest to widest]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the verb to eat",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the verb *to eat*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is bhakṣaṇaṁ? (Food.) Is it a particular dish or the whole category? (The whole category.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ML-C92-hottal",
      "sequence": 3260,
      "title": "ഹോട്ടൽ (hōṭṭal) — an eating place",
      "headword": "ഹോട്ടൽ",
      "romanization": "hōṭṭal",
      "gloss": "an eating place — a borrowed word that does not mean in Kerala what it means in English",
      "script": "malayalam",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:5f2ddfc27a3ef459",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the word for food. Say the word for a shop."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ഹോട്ടൽ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ഹോട്ടൽ — hōṭṭal — a place that serves food."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Read it aloud and you will hear an English word inside it. Do not trust it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In Kerala a ഹോട്ടൽ (hōṭṭal) is ordinarily somewhere you go to eat — a tea-and-meals place, the sort with a board outside listing what is on today. A bed is not what the word promises."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ഹോട്ടലിൽ — hōṭṭalil — at the eating place. The in-ending attaches the ordinary way."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way: a borrowed word with its own life",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A word that crosses into another language stops belonging to the one it came from. It keeps whichever part of its meaning the borrowers needed and lets the rest go, and afterwards nobody has to consult the original."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That is worth holding on to as a habit, because this will not be the last borrowed word you meet here, and the one thing you must not do is assume it still means what it means at home."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You have already met borrowings that behaved themselves — കാപ്പി is coffee and മേശ came from Portuguese. ഹോട്ടൽ (hōṭṭal) is the first one in this book that would mislead you if you trusted the source."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "hōṭṭal",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *hōṭṭal*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "what you would expect to find there",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: what you would expect to find there]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "hōṭṭalil",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *hōṭṭalil*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "what the English word would have led you to expect instead",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: what the English word would have led you to expect instead]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is a hōṭṭal in Kerala? (A place to eat.) Should a borrowed word be trusted to keep its old meaning? (No.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
