    {
      "id": "ML-R92-eating-out-recall",
      "sequence": 3280,
      "title": "(eating out) (puṟattŭ kaḻikkal enna ōrmma) — from cold",
      "headword": "(eating out)",
      "romanization": "puṟattŭ kaḻikkal enna ōrmma",
      "gloss": "cold retrieval of the food word, the eating place, the bill, and the rule about trusting a borrowed word",
      "script": "malayalam",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:45619b3feb51357c",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Close the lessons before this one. Say food, then say the place that serves it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "The exchange — the shape of a meal out",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ഹോട്ടലിൽ — at the eating place. ഭക്ഷണം (bhakṣaṇaṁ) — food. ബിൽ. എത്ര രൂപ? — the bill. How many rupees?"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three new words, and the question at the end is one you have had since the shopping chapter."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way: two borrowings, two different fates",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "came from English",
                "means here"
              ],
              "columns": 3,
              "rowCount": 2,
              "utterances": [
                "ഹോട്ടൽ (hōṭṭal). came from English: hotel. means here: a place to eat.",
                "ബിൽ. came from English: bill. means here: the bill."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One changed and one did not, and that is the whole reason they sit in the same chapter. A word that crosses into another language stops belonging to the one it came from — it keeps whichever part of its meaning the borrowers needed and lets the rest go."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So the habit is: check each borrowing, do not trust the family. Recognising the English inside a word tells you how to pronounce it and nothing reliable about what it means."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The food words, meanwhile, finally have a roof. അരി, ചോറ്, ഊണ് were all particular; ഭക്ഷണം (bhakṣaṇaṁ) is the category they belong to — and a course can easily leave you able to order lunch and unable to say the word food."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
